at competitive", "A Trump-aligned super PAC ad misidentifying Nez drew damaging October coverage"], "rationale": "Crane's incumbency, the district's Republican lean, and his large money advantage make him the clear favorite, with most raters at Likely R and markets pricing Nez near 25 percent. A favorable Democratic environment and a misfired GOP attack ad keep the expected margin narrower than the district's raw lean alone would suggest.", "watch": "Fresh fall polling and whether the attack-ad blunder keeps resonating could push the race toward Tossup if the Democratic wave builds.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 51 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Eli Crane (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Rep. Eli Crane seeks a third term in Arizona's 2nd District in a rematch against Democrat Jonathan Nez, a former Navajo Nation president, with Libertarian Curtis Goodwin also on the ballot. The seat leans Republican by about seven points, and Crane holds a large financial advantage, having raised roughly $9.5 million to Nez's $3.5 million. Most race raters call the contest Likely Republican, though a few rate it Lean Republican and one calls it a Tossup.\n\nLimited August polling showed Crane ahead by two to eleven points, with the newest data now two months old. Prediction markets give Nez roughly a one-in-four chance. The race drew wide attention in October when a Trump-aligned super PAC ad attacking Nez used a photo of the wrong man, drawing criticism across outlets. A strong Democratic midterm environment keeps the district in play, but Crane remains favored.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1703, "margin": -5.61}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2296, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2331, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2721, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1924, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2064, "margin": -5.0}, {"date":